<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>O’Neale is paid <strong>$10.88m</strong> because he is a wing defender who shoots threes in volume, and that combination holds its price into a player’s thirties.</p>
<ul>
<li>He has taken more than 400 threes in a season and made them at league average or better, which is enough for any club to play him.</li>
<li>He defends the opposing team’s best wing, which is the job clubs find hardest to fill cheaply.</li>
<li>He went undrafted and spent two seasons abroad first, so his first NBA contract came at 24 and cost <a href="/teams/utah-jazz/">Utah</a> very little.</li>
<li>He scores in single figures, which is why the number sits in this range instead of higher.</li>
<li>Every dollar of the contract is guaranteed, so the club carries the risk of any decline.</li>
</ul>
<p>He earns slightly more than <a href="/players/kon-knueppel/">Kon Knueppel</a>, the fourth pick in the 2025 draft, who is twelve years younger.</p>

